# HTML tables are deliberately short; all detailed rows remain downloadable.
def table(df):return df.to_html(index=False,escape=True,float_format=lambda v:f'{v:.2f}',border=0,classes='data')
def pct_table(df,cols):
 df=df.copy()
 for c in cols:df[c]=df[c]*100
 return table(df)
def img(name,caption):return f'<figure><img src="{name}.png" alt="{html.escape(caption)}"><figcaption>{caption} <a href="{name}.pdf">PDF</a></figcaption></figure>'
parts=['''<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>What does an informative contrast actually do?</title><style>
body{font:17px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif;color:#20313e;background:#f7f9fb;margin:0}main{max-width:1160px;margin:auto;padding:32px 35px 70px}h1{font-size:38px;line-height:1.16}h2{margin-top:42px;font-size:26px}h3{font-size:20px}p{max-width:1010px}.lede{font-size:21px}.box{background:#e7f0f7;border-left:5px solid #286c9f;padding:18px 23px;margin:25px 0}.caution{background:#fff2dd;border-color:#be8b39}figure{margin:28px 0;background:white;padding:12px;border:1px solid #dce3e8;border-radius:9px}img{width:100%;height:auto}figcaption{font-size:14px;color:#536775}table{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px;width:100%;background:white}th,td{padding:9px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #dfe5e9;text-align:right}th:first-child,td:first-child{text-align:left}th{background:#e9eef2}a{color:#1a6294}summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:650}details{padding:13px;border:1px solid #d8e2e9;background:white;margin:12px 0}.card{background:white;border:1px solid #dce3e8;padding:20px;margin:18px 0;border-radius:9px}.muted{font-size:14px;color:#536775}.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:18px}blockquote{margin:12px 0;padding-left:15px;border-left:3px solid #b5c9d8;font-size:16px}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;word-wrap:break-word;font-size:13px}.scroll{overflow-x:auto}nav{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:15px;font-size:14px}@media(max-width:750px){main{padding:20px}.grid{display:block}h1{font-size:30px}}
</style></head><body><main><p class="muted">Interpretable probe · Existing-data investigation · 20 October 2026</p><h1>What does an informative contrast actually do?</h1><p class="lede">A contrast can reveal a useful difference without telling the judge which side of the classification boundary the target belongs on. Our previous “contrast” label combines these two questions.</p><nav><a href="#main">Main findings</a><a href="#boundary">Boundary distance</a><a href="#examples">Real examples</a><a href="#interventions">Existing interventions</a><a href="#limits">Robustness</a><a href="#next">Next study</a><a href="#methods">Methods and files</a></nav>
<div class="box"><strong>The clearest new finding:</strong> in contrast-labelled toxicity cases, 83.2% of harmful flips move <em>toward</em> the neighbour’s class, not away. The dominant observed error is therefore compatible with following an inappropriate neighbour label; it is not predominantly excessive repulsion. This describes output direction, not a proven internal mechanism. Even when the neighbour’s dataset label and probe prediction agree, 85.7% of harmful toxicity contrast flips move toward that shared class.</div>
<h2 id="main">1. We were mixing up four different things</h2><ol><li><strong>A meaningful difference exists</strong> between target and neighbour — what our annotators labelled.</li><li><strong>The neighbour has a different gold class</strong> from the target — known retrospectively, not available at deployment.</li><li><strong>The judge’s score moves away</strong> from the neighbour’s labelled class — an observed probability change.</li><li><strong>The target should cross the decision threshold</strong> — a further claim that a difference alone may not justify.</li></ol><p>They need not coincide. A safer target compared with a labelled-safe neighbour can reinforce the <em>same</em> safe class. Conversely, a target can be less severe than an unsafe neighbour and still be unsafe. Meaningful contrast is also often about intent, refusal versus compliance, or factual support rather than a single severity scale.</p>''']
parts+=[img('contrast_decomposition','Toxicity 4, all ten outcome judges. Score movement uses the original probability-only outputs; harmful flips are correct control → wrong NN at the same 50 cutoff.')]
parts+=['''<p>The non-contrast and contrast groups are not comparable mixtures: the neighbour’s label matches the target gold label in <strong>91.2%</strong> versus <strong>49.0%</strong> of their weighted rows. That difference makes the crude contrast-versus-no-contrast accuracy gap a poor causal estimate of the value of contrast.</p><p>The two directions of harm both matter. Among harmful direct-judge contrast flips on toxicity, 40.2% are false positives produced by moving toward an unsafe-labelled neighbour, and 43.0% are false negatives produced by moving toward a safe-labelled neighbour. In the actual probe50 → judge50 pipeline, the latter accounts for 58.0% of harms: a benign reference can make the judge veto a correct probe warning. These percentages describe the mix of harms, not conditional error rates or global precision/recall changes.</p><h2>2. Contrast is associated with less following of the neighbour’s class</h2><p>A control decision that already agrees with the neighbour can only flip away; one that disagrees can only flip toward it. The following analysis uses these separate opportunities as denominators.</p>''',img('opportunity_rates','Conditional rates, with approximate target-cluster 95% intervals. “Correct-class neighbour” means its gold label matches the target gold, not that its text is a valid justification.')]
parts+=['''<p>This is consistent with a contrast making the judge less willing to copy a neighbour. That can prevent harm when the neighbour’s class is wrong for the target, but also lose useful corrections when its class is right. The associations remain after adjusting for model/backbone/dataset, score margins, cosine similarity and cue agreement. They weaken substantially when comparing the same target across backbones; the toxicity intervals then all include zero. We therefore cannot attribute these differences to contrast alone.</p><h2 id="boundary">3. Your distancing intuition is plausible — but it is about relative position</h2><p>Contrast-labelled pairs are closer to the probe’s 50% boundary on average: their mean within-probe absolute-logit-margin rank is <strong>0.36</strong>, compared with <strong>0.55</strong> without contrast. They also have lower cosine-similarity ranks. This supports a connection with ambiguous local regions, but neither cosine nor a contrast annotation measures the judge’s true semantic decision boundary.</p>''',img('ordering_not_boundary','Both toy worlds agree that the neighbour is positive and the target is less severe. They disagree about the target’s class. A single anchor does not identify the boundary or the target’s absolute distance from it.'),'''<table><thead><tr><th>Neighbour’s label</th><th>Target relative to neighbour</th><th>What follows under a one-dimensional monotone rule?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Positive</td><td>More positive / severe</td><td>Supports the same positive class</td></tr><tr><td>Positive</td><td>Less positive / severe</td><td>Different class is not established</td></tr><tr><td>Negative</td><td>Less positive / severe</td><td>Supports the same negative class</td></tr><tr><td>Negative</td><td>More positive / severe</td><td>Different class is not established</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="muted">This is an explanatory toy assumption, not a claim that toxicity, factual support or an LLM decision rule lies on one scalar axis. It explains why detecting a meaningful difference is not enough to justify switching classes.</p>''', img('boundary_patterns','Quintiles are ranked separately within each backbone/dataset using saved probe probabilities. Left: the net effect differs strongly with retrospective label alignment. Right: conditioning on the opportunity to flip makes clear that not every kind of flip becomes more frequent near the probe boundary.')]
parts+=['''<p>For a linear logistic probe, distance to its own 50% hyperplane is |logit(p)| / ||w|| in the specified feature coordinates. Ranking |logit(p)| within a probe removes the unknown constant norm. It does <em>not</em> recover comparable distances across probes or an LLM’s boundary. Exact saved probe coefficients and second-neighbour retrieval metadata could not be refreshed because SSH timed out; this report uses the verified local score exports. Twenty-seven of 25,000 contexts have saturated probabilities, so the transformation clips them at 10⁻¹².</p><p>Nor does greater cosine similarity remove the contrast problem: among toxicity contrasts whose neighbour label differs from target gold, NN accuracy changes remain about −9 to −11 points across all five cosine-rank bins. Those whose labels align improve by about 11–15 points across the bins. This is a retrospective diagnostic, not a gold-label-based deployment policy; it shows why similarity alone need not tell us whether the neighbour is relevant in the right way.</p><p><strong>A useful interpretation:</strong> a neighbour supplies a local reference point. The judge still has to decide which difference is relevant, which direction it points in, and whether it is large enough to change the class. Our binary “important contrast” annotation records only the first of those.</p><h2 id="examples">4. Real examples distinguish these possibilities</h2><p>These are exploratory examples from an outcome-stratified audit, not a prevalence sample. “Correct” means agreement with the dataset’s recorded label. Later expressed explanations came from a different probability-first prompt; even a preserved decision does not establish the original model’s internal reasoning.</p>''']
parts += ['<div class="box"><strong>Not just marginal nudges:</strong> 62.4% of harmful toxicity contrast flips start at a reported control probability ≤10 or ≥90; 29.9% jump exactly between 0 and 100. Helpful flips also often start from extreme scores (52.0%), with 29.4% exact endpoint reversals. These are reported scores, not demonstrated calibrated confidence. For comparison, 73.3% of unchanged contrast cases also start at extreme scores: flips are relatively less extreme, but are still often far from 50. A local probe-boundary account does not imply that the judge itself starts near its 50 cutoff.</div>', img('confidence_jumps','Helpful and harmful flips have mean score changes of about 73 and 74 points, respectively. The predominance of toward-label harms persists at common judge cutoffs 25, 40, 50, 60 and 75 (81–85% on toxicity), so it is not solely an exact-50 tie artefact.')]
