parts+=['</details><h2 id="interventions">5. Existing interventions add useful evidence</h2>',img('existing_interventions','Left: controlled component removals, five previously selected high-benefit backbone/judge pairs. Right: 2-NN versus 1-NN on the same test cases, six local judges. Annotations describe the original 1-NN pair in both analyses.')]
parts+=['''<p>In the five-pair ablations, removing the neighbour’s label from otherwise complete explanations did not improve contrast cases overall. Removing both label and prediction reduced toxicity accuracy by <strong>1.61 points</strong> relative to full 1-NN (95% interval −2.60 to −0.62). Thus, simply removing labelled information is not a general fix.</p><p>Within the retrospectively identified opposite-gold-label contrast subset, text + prediction outperformed full 1-NN by about <strong>1.10 points</strong>, while label-only was worse by <strong>2.34 points</strong>. This is consistent with a misleading label sometimes overpowering a useful textual distinction. It is not a deployable rule: target gold labels are unavailable when deciding whether to show the neighbour.</p><p>The second-neighbour analysis is more encouraging. Across all ten datasets, accuracy improved by <strong>3.05 points</strong> in contrast cases (95% interval 2.47–3.64), versus <strong>0.37 points</strong> without contrast (0.16–0.58). On the four toxicity datasets the gains were <strong>1.58</strong> and <strong>0.97 points</strong>, respectively. The toxicity difference between these gains is not clearly resolved; see the paired interaction table below. An equal-cell sensitivity gives the same broad result: across all ten datasets, 2-NN gains 2.62 points in contrast cases versus 0.37 without contrast, a 2.25-point difference (95% interval 1.61–2.89). This reduces the concern that the difference is only due to the two groups containing different dataset/judge mixtures.</p>''']
inter=read('second_nn_interaction');parts+=[pct_table(inter,['difference','lo','hi'])]
parts+=['''<p>High Stakes (+5.79 points), WildJailbreak (+7.69) and FAVA (+4.50) contribute to the all-dataset contrast benefit; ToxicChat is nearly unchanged (+0.24). This is not a uniformly toxicity-specific result. All four displayed example decisions also persist with 2-NN; the average improvement does not mean adding a reference fixes each illustrative mistake. Importantly, the second-neighbour benefit varies by judge: on toxicity contrast cases, GLM gains <strong>6.29 points</strong>, while Qwen122 loses <strong>1.67</strong>. A second nearest neighbour is not necessarily an example of the opposite class, and its labels are not in the local score export. We cannot yet claim that bracketing the boundary explains this improvement.</p>''',img('judge_heterogeneity','The left panel uses ten judges; the right panel uses only the six local judges with completed 2-NN outputs. Do not compare the two panel averages as if their judge populations were identical.')]
parts+=['<h2 id="limits">6. Robustness: what survives, and what does not</h2><p>The majority-labelled contrast group has a small positive average accuracy effect under fixed50 decisions, but not under the frozen tuned policies. Its descriptive net effect is therefore policy-sensitive.</p>']
pol=[]
for policy,label in [('judge50_direct','Direct judge50'),('pipeline50','Probe50 + judge50')]:
 for co in ['all10','toxicity4']:
  r=s.query("policy==@policy and cohort==@co and annotator=='majority' and similarity=='all' and contrast=='yes'").iloc[0];pol.append(dict(cohort=co,policy=label,gain=r.gain,lo=r.gain_lo,hi=r.gain_hi))
for co in ['all10','toxicity4']:
 r=anc.query("cohort==@co and anchor=='three_anchor_average' and contrast=='yes'").iloc[0];pol.append(dict(cohort=co,policy='Three shared tuned anchors',gain=r.gain,lo=r.lo,hi=r.hi))
parts+=[pct_table(pd.DataFrame(pol),['gain','lo','hi'])]
parts+=['''<p>Under the shared tuned anchors, about <strong>83%</strong> of harmful toxicity contrast flips still move toward the neighbour’s label. Zero judge thresholds are retained exactly as frozen; those cells cannot flip and dilute net effects. The three-anchor rows average over policies, not three independent datasets.</p><p>The “contrast is less beneficial than no contrast” association appears with each annotator. But unanimously labelled contrast is a more difficult subset and has slightly negative net fixed50 gain on toxicity (−0.70 points). The annotation consensus rule changes case composition; it is not simply a confidence knob for the same population. Across all 25,000 contexts, the three annotators agree exactly on important contrast in 66.2%, compared with 77.8% for labelled similarity. Pairwise contrast kappas are 0.44–0.58. That measurement uncertainty is another reason not to treat the current contrast label as a precise mechanism detector. Excluding the three judges used as annotators and using common-valid cases are included in the downloadable sensitivity results.</p><details><summary>Adjusted opportunity-conditioned associations</summary><p>Coefficient units below are percentage points. The same-target analysis compares differences across available backbones for a fixed model and target, additionally controlling cell effects. It still does not randomise retrieval or contrast.</p>''']
r=risk.query("annotator=='majority' and term=='contrast'")[['cohort','possible_direction','flip_outcome','spec','estimate','lo','hi']];parts+=[pct_table(r,['estimate','lo','hi']),'</details>']
parts+=['''<h2>7. Relevant research supports the distinction, not a causal conclusion</h2>''']
for l in json.load(open(R/'literature.json'))['sources']:
 parts += [f'<p><a href="{html.escape(l["url"])}">{html.escape(l["title"])}</a>: {html.escape(l["finding"])} {html.escape(l["connection"])} <span class="muted">{html.escape(l["limit"])}</span></p>']
parts+=['''<h2 id="next">8. The next experiment should test boundary information directly</h2><p>The most useful extension is not another broad “contrast yes/no” label. Separate <strong>direction</strong> from <strong>sufficiency to change class</strong>. A contrast can be meaningful yet insufficient; it can also reinforce the same class. Ask independent annotators what classification-relevant difference exists, whether it makes the target more or less likely to be positive relative to the neighbour, and whether the supplied information supports a different class or leaves it uncertain. Keep target gold labels and judge outcomes hidden.</p><p>Then compare the existing 1-NN and 2-nearest conditions with <strong>two comparable training examples, one from each class</strong>. Retrieve using training examples only, not the target’s gold label. A matched pair differing in one relevant feature is a cleaner test than two arbitrary opposite-class examples. Control prompt length and example order, and preserve the frozen threshold comparisons. Evaluate corrections and harms separately, including same-class pairs and difficult disagreement cases. This would test whether a better-specified local boundary helps, rather than merely adding more text or balancing labels.</p><p>No new model calls were launched during this investigation. The current evidence supports a local-reference-point account, but does not establish psychological distancing as the internal cause.</p>
<h2 id="methods">Methods, provenance and scope</h2><ul><li><strong>Main data:</strong> 25,000 test contexts, 15,915 distinct target-text clusters, 240,524 paired-valid judge–context rows across ten judges, five backbones and ten datasets. The toxicity subset is Aegis, BeaverTails, ToxicChat and ToxiGen. Missing API results remain excluded under the original eligibility rule requiring valid control, saliency and NN scores. No missing judge calls were regenerated.</li><li><strong>Primary outcome:</strong> original probability-only judge predictions at ≥50, before probe referral gating. This isolates changes in the judge’s output. Pipeline50 and three frozen shared threshold anchors are reported separately. No thresholds were retuned in this investigation.</li><li><strong>Weights:</strong> original inverse inclusion weights, normalized to equal total mass within each judge/backbone/dataset cell. Conditional subgroup statistics retain those weights, so subgroup composition varies. They are not equal-weight subgroup-cell macro averages. Supplemental F1 files calculate F1 per cell; accuracy and F1 are not interchangeable.</li><li><strong>Intervals:</strong> approximate 95% target-cluster sandwich intervals. They account for repeated target texts across judges/backbones and anchors, conditional on the existing labels and selection. They do not include annotation, model-training or threshold-selection uncertainty, and are not multiplicity-corrected. This is exploratory analysis of an already examined test set.</li><li><strong>Definitions:</strong> signed attraction is (2 × neighbour label − 1) × (NN score − control score). Positive means movement toward the neighbour’s labelled class. In binary decisions, a toward flip helps exactly when the neighbour label matches target gold; this identity is not independent causal evidence.</li><li><strong>Neighbour prediction:</strong> the prompt supplies a binary probe class under the original probe’s saved cutoff, not a continuous neighbour confidence or measured distance. It should not be interpreted as a 50% probability margin.</li><li><strong>Annotation:</strong> three existing local annotators; majority yes/no is primary. The original prompt explicitly allowed both labelled similarity and important contrast to be yes, and did not require contrast to imply opposite classes.</li><li><strong>Qualitative audit:</strong> 32 deterministically selected candidates, eight per direction × outcome stratum, with at most two per dataset and distinct target texts within stratum. Eight short pairs and their available traces were closely reviewed; four are shown above. All judge outcomes remain in the candidate export. No frequency estimates are inferred from this small outcome-visible audit.</li><li><strong>Geometry limit:</strong> saved score-derived within-probe margin ranks are available. Exact coefficient-normalized cross-probe distances and the second-neighbour class composition were not recovered while SSH was unavailable. Cosine distance is angular similarity, not distance to a hyperplane.</li><li><strong>Intervention scope:</strong> ablation results come from five previously selected high-NN-benefit pairs; 2-NN results cover all six local judges. Neither covers all ten judges. Original full-NN annotations are reused to define strata, not to assert that altered prompts contain identical mechanisms.</li></ul>
<details><summary>Download tables, figures, exact examples and verification</summary><ul>''']
